# Editing the search's long-lived scripts does nothing until you restart them

`scripts/search/controller.py`, `watchdog.py`, `pool_manager.py`, and
`worker.py` are each launched ONCE per SLURM job and then run an internal
`while True` polling loop for days (`controller.sbatch`/`watchdog.sbatch`
self-chain around day 5; `worker_pool*.sbatch` self-chains too). Python
imports each of these files into memory a single time at process startup --
editing the file on disk afterward has **zero effect on the already-running
process**. This bit us for real on 2026-07-31: `worker.py` was edited to
pass a new CLI flag, but the live worker pool (started hours earlier) kept
launching training subprocesses without it, so a bug fix silently never took
effect until this was diagnosed by noticing a failure happened chronologically
*after* the "fix" was deployed.

**`main.py` (and anything else worker.py/controller.py only ever invokes via
a fresh `subprocess.run(...)`) is NOT subject to this** -- each ticket execs
a brand-new interpreter that reads the current file from disk, so edits to
`main.py`/`training/*.py` take effect on the very next ticket automatically.

## After editing any of controller.py / watchdog.py / worker.py / pool_manager.py

1. Identify the exact running job(s) that would need to pick up the change
   (`squeue`, cross-reference against `matrix_month_controller` /
   `matrix_month_watchdog` / `matrix_month_workers*` job names).
2. `scancel` that job. If it's the worker pool, first manually force-reclaim
   any tickets it had claimed (`reclaim_stale_claims(paths, max_age_seconds=0)`
   from `scripts.search.search_state`) rather than waiting out the default
   3-hour stale-lease timeout.
3. Resubmit the corresponding `*.sbatch` file. Expect the recently-edited
   file to possibly hit the stale-filesystem-cache null-bytes bug
   (`.cursor/rules/stale-file-cache-null-bytes.mdc`) on the first resubmit
   attempt -- verify 0 null bytes locally, delete+rewrite to force a new
   inode if it does, and resubmit again.
4. Verify the restarted job is actually healthy (correct GPU allocation,
   `.err` free of tracebacks, queue actively claiming/producing fresh
   results) before considering the fix live.

Never assume a code change to one of these four files is live just because
the edit was saved and tests pass -- tests only prove the code is correct,
not that the right process is running it.
